# PRACTICAL: Floyd-Warshall Algorithm

## Aim
To write a Python program to implement the Floyd-Warshall algorithm for finding the shortest paths between all pairs of vertices in a weighted graph.

## Introduction
The Floyd-Warshall algorithm is a shortest-path algorithm used in graph theory. It finds the shortest distances between every pair of vertices in a weighted graph.

It follows the Dynamic Programming approach and works with directed or undirected weighted graphs. It can also handle negative edge weights, but not negative weight cycles.

## Algorithm
1. Start with the adjacency matrix of the graph.
2. Initialize the distance matrix using the given graph.
3. Consider each vertex as an intermediate vertex.
4. Check whether the path through the intermediate vertex is shorter.
5. Update the distance matrix if a shorter path is found.
6. Repeat the process for all vertices.
7. Display the final shortest distance matrix.

## Time Complexity
- Time Complexity: O(V³)
- Space Complexity: O(V²)

Where V represents the number of vertices in the graph.

## Applications
- Finding shortest routes in transportation networks.
- Network routing and communication systems.
- Road map navigation.
- Graph analysis.
- Transportation planning.

## Conclusion
The Floyd-Warshall algorithm successfully finds the shortest distances between all pairs of vertices in a weighted graph. It uses Dynamic Programming to improve paths through intermediate vertices and produces the final shortest distance matrix.
